 normal-stretch (x1=0.71) SN Ia peaking at day -2.3 with chi2 per point 0.87 and the highest model weight 0.41. The very red g-r color is naturally accounted for by high reddening c=0.40, and the stamp classifier (p_real 0.82) supports a real compact transient rather than subtraction noise.", "hypothesis": "ia", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfr", "day": 4, "direction": "above", "flux": 70, "why": "A post-peak normal Ia must fade in r over the next week, so persistent peak-level flux would break the decline prediction."}, "weakest_point": "The fit requires extreme reddening c=0.40 at galactic latitude 5 deg where Milky Way dust is large and shows an unusually flat ~0.4 mag r-band rise over eight pre-peak days for a normal Ia."}, {"argument": "The light curve shows a slow rise and week-long flat red plateau at r about 19.2 with g minus r about 1.4, consistent with a stripped-envelope supernova at redshift about 0.15 viewed after maximum during iron-line blanketing and cooling. The Ib/c model fits all detections and limits with chi2 per point 0.79 and the difference stamp is a compact real source with no field-wide subtraction failure.", "hypothesis": "ibc", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfr", "day": 4, "direction": "above", "flux": 110, "why": "A post-peak Ib/c tail at this redshift must continue fading below about 75 microjansky, so a brightening to well above current in r would rule out the model."}, "weakest_point": "The peak is placed 22 days before the data with no observed rise or fall, so the classification rests on a flat 9-day tail that is unusually non-declining for a normal Ib/c."}, {"argument": "The r band has held near 19.2 mag from day -10.5 to -1.5 with chi2 per point 1.19 for a z 0.092 Type II peaking at day -18.7, matching a post-maximum plateau about two to three weeks after explosion. The persistently faint g near 20.6 mag giving g-r near 1.4 mag matches a cooling red hydrogen envelope on the plateau rather than a blue rising phase.", "hypothesis": "ii", "refuted_if": {"band": "ztfr", "day": 4, "direction": "below", "flux": 50, "why": "A hydrogen recombination plateau 19 to 23 days after peak should remain near 75 microjansky in r with only slow decline so a fall this faint requires rapid cooling inconsistent with sustained plateau "}, "weakest_point": "At Galactic latitude 5 deg foreground extinction and crowding are severe so the very red color and luminosity scale at z near 0.09 cannot securely establish an extragalactic hydrogen plateau."}], "weights": {"ia": 0.5432, "ibc": 0.1379, "ii": 0.1867, "null": 0.1322}, "wording": {"term": "about as likely as not", "text": "It is about as likely as not that the distant space flash will still be fairly bright in red light 4 days from now.", "words_match": true}}, "prev": "e140f4909777acdc7291e4f48d25fa2d7321dcdab89450689c00d8f9eb6cc225", "seq": 11, "time": "2018-06-21T21:49:58+00:00", "v": 2}
